  "provenance": {
    "pattern": "breakbeat (nu-skool and big beat): a funk break's syncopated kick under a firm two-and-four snare at 125-135, sixteenth hats with an open hat at the end of the phrase",
    "knowledge": "public knowledge of the genre: the pattern as drummers, producers and tutorials commonly write it; no recording was sampled, transcribed from audio or mined",
    "note": "Breaks is not a family in genres.json any more; it lives under drum and bass, which is where the break came to rest. Two bars: kick one, two-a, three-and; then one, one-and, three-e, four-and. Snare on two and four with ghosts on two-a and four-a. Closed sixteenths, accented on the eighths and lightly swung, and a loose hat opening on the last and of each second bar. The bass is a syncopated funk line inside a fifth."
  }
